eft and bottom-left and 11 captured stones, and Black kept only the wall on the F and G files.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "The 12k's F5 lost 6.1 points and moved Black's lead from -0.6 to -6.7. The engine wanted F4. It was the first big mistake of the game, and White was clearly ahead for a moment."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "Opus 5.5 played E5, which lost 8.8 points and turned Black's lead from -3.2 to +5.6. The engine's choice was C2. White gave the advantage straight back."}, {"turn": 14, "text": "E7 was Opus 5.5's worst move, losing 9.8 points and sending Black's lead from -3.4 to +6.5. The engine's choice was D2. After E7 White has a chain of stones on the D and E files, but the analysis says it should have gone elsewhere."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "The 12k's F7 was the worst move of the game, losing 11.7 points. It took Black's lead from +6.5 to -5.3, and the engine wanted C2. This was the last big swing, and White held the lead for the rest of the game."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "From move 27 the two sides traded moves in the left corner. Black's B7 lost 3.9 points (-7.9 to -11.8), and Opus 5.5's B6 answered by taking 3.9 back (-11.8 to -7.8). The same trade repeated until move 46, and Black's stones there ended up captured."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and flood-fills each group to print its stones and liberties, flagging groups with two or fewer liberties as DANGER. It evaluates the current position without trying moves or looking ahead, and both runs only show group and liberty counts.", "stop": false}}